    "result_text": "The change is implemented and the full test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nModified `AssignCustomerCode` (the `assignCode` step in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline) to de-duplicate the customerCode on collision:\n\n`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java`\n\n- Injected `OwnerRepository` (following the existing pattern in `RequireUniqueIdentity` / `AssignNamesakeCount`) and collected the set of existing owners' `customerCode`s via `findAll()`.\n- Computes the base `<REGION>-<HASH8>` code from `CustomerCodes.forOwner(owner)`, then if it collides, appends `-<n>` starting at `n = 2` and increments until unique \u2014 so the smallest `n \u2265 2` that resolves the collision wins.\n- The `customerCode` set on the owner is the de-duplicated value.\n\nThis preserves `CustomerCodes.localityOf`, which reads the region as the substring before the *first* `-`, so an appended `-2` suffix doesn't affect locality derivation.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `AcceptanceTest.coreCustomerCodesStayUnique` passes (distinct owners get distinct customerCodes).\n- Full `./mvnw test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.\n\nOne note: the repo's black-box test can't force an actual hash collision (that needs an identical normalized telephone + last name, which telephone-uniqueness blocks upstream), so it asserts the uniqueness guarantee rather than the literal `-2` suffix. The suffix logic itself is exercised whenever a genuine collision occurs.",
